def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  return d
E=[]
# --- part 1: breakthroughs that rewired the world
E+=[
X('newton-principia-1687','Newton\'s Principia: one law for the apple and the Moon',H,'confirmed',1,1687,['documentary','scientific publication','manuscripts'],
 "In July 1687 Isaac Newton published Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica (Mathematical Principles of Natural Philosophy). The astronomer Edmond Halley encouraged the work and paid for its printing. In it Newton set out three laws of motion and a law of universal gravitation: every mass attracts every other mass with a force that weakens with the square of the distance. With one set of rules he explained falling objects on Earth, the orbits of the planets described by Kepler, the paths of comets and the tides. The Principia became the foundation of physics for more than two centuries, until Einstein's relativity refined it.",
 [('The apple','The famous apple story comes from Newton himself, told late in life to his friend William Stukeley and others. Historians accept that he may have used it as an illustration, but the idea that an apple hit him on the head is a later embellishment.'),
  ('Who thought of it first?','Robert Hooke claimed he had suggested the inverse-square law to Newton, and the two quarrelled bitterly. Newton also fought a long dispute with Gottfried Leibniz over who invented calculus; most historians now say both developed it independently.'),
  ('"The last of the magicians"','Newton wrote far more on alchemy, biblical prophecy and ancient chronology than on physics. He made his own translation of the alchemical Emerald Tablet, and in a note he reckoned the world would not end before 2060. After buying many of these papers, the economist John Maynard Keynes called him "not the first of the age of reason" but "the last of the magicians". Historians debate how far these interests shaped his science.')],
 ['wiki:Philosophiæ_Naturalis_Principia_Mathematica','wiki:Isaac_Newton','wiki:Isaac_Newton\'s_occult_studies'],img='Isaac Newton',
 changed='1687: Principia published. 1915: Einstein\'s general relativity refines Newtonian gravity. 1936: Newton\'s alchemical and religious papers auctioned, revealing their extent.'),
X('faraday-induction-1831','Faraday and electromagnetic induction: the birth of the power age',H,'confirmed',2,1831,['experimental','laboratory notebooks','scientific publication'],
 "Michael Faraday, the son of a blacksmith, had little formal schooling. He educated himself while apprenticed to a London bookbinder and became assistant to the chemist Humphry Davy at the Royal Institution. In August 1831 he wound two coils of wire on an iron ring and found that switching a current on or off in one coil produced a brief current in the other. He then showed that moving a magnet near a wire generates electricity. This electromagnetic induction is the principle behind electric generators and transformers, which supply almost all the world's electricity today. Faraday also described lines of force (fields), an idea James Clerk Maxwell later turned into mathematics.",
 [('Joseph Henry','The American scientist Joseph Henry discovered induction independently at about the same time, but Faraday published first.'),
  ('"One day, sir, you may tax it"','A popular story says a politician, often named as William Gladstone, asked Faraday what use electricity was, and he replied that one day the government would tax it. No contemporary source for the exchange has been found, and historians treat it as apocryphal.'),
  ('Faraday and the table-turners','In 1853, during the craze for spiritualist séances, Faraday tested "table-turning" with a simple device and concluded that the sitters were moving the table themselves through unconscious muscle movements. Spiritualists rejected his explanation.')],
 ['wiki:Michael_Faraday','wiki:Electromagnetic_induction','wiki:Table-turning'],img='Michael Faraday'),
X('telegraph-1844','"What hath God wrought": the electric telegraph',H,'confirmed',2,1844,['documentary','technological'],
 "In the 1830s several inventors built electrical telegraphs. In Britain William Cooke and Charles Wheatstone put a needle telegraph into commercial use on a railway in 1838. In the United States Samuel Morse, a painter turned inventor, worked with Alfred Vail on a system using a code of dots and dashes. On 24 May 1844 Morse sent the message 'What hath God wrought', a line from the Bible, from Washington to Baltimore. Within decades telegraph wires crossed continents, and from 1866 a lasting cable ran under the Atlantic. For the first time news could travel faster than a horse or a ship.",
 [('The Victorian internet','Some historians compare the telegraph to the internet: it brought instant long-distance messages, codes and ciphers, online-style romances and new kinds of fraud. Critics of the comparison say it was costly and used mainly by businesses, governments and newspapers.'),
  ('A cable that lasted weeks','The first transatlantic cable of 1858 carried a message from Queen Victoria to President Buchanan, but it failed within weeks; its electrician applied too high a voltage while trying to make it work. Some at the time suspected the whole thing was a hoax.'),
  ('Wires to the spirit world','The rise of spiritualism, with its rapping spirits, came in the same years. Believers described séances as a kind of spiritual telegraph, and one New York spiritualist paper of the 1850s was called the Spiritual Telegraph.')],
 ['wiki:Electrical_telegraph','wiki:Samuel_Morse','wiki:Transatlantic_telegraph_cable'],img='Samuel Morse',
 changed='1838: Cooke and Wheatstone telegraph in service. 1844: Morse\'s Washington–Baltimore line. 1858: first transatlantic cable fails. 1866: lasting transatlantic cable.'),
X('broad-street-cholera-1854','The Broad Street pump: John Snow maps a killer',H,'confirmed',1,1854,['epidemiological','documentary','maps'],
 "In late August 1854 cholera broke out in Soho, London, and more than 600 people died within weeks. Most doctors believed cholera spread through 'miasma', bad air from filth. The physician John Snow suspected contaminated water. He recorded where the victims lived and found they clustered around a public water pump on Broad Street; people who drank other water, such as workers at a nearby brewery, were mostly spared. On 8 September he persuaded local officials to remove the pump's handle. Snow's map of the outbreak is often seen as a founding moment of epidemiology. The bacterium that causes cholera was described by Filippo Pacini in 1854 and studied further by Robert Koch in 1884.",
 [('Did removing the handle stop it?','Snow himself noted that the outbreak was already declining when the handle was removed, perhaps because many people had fled or died. The story of a single dramatic act is a simplification.'),
  ('The baby\'s nappies','A local curate, Henry Whitehead, set out to disprove Snow but was won over. He traced the probable source to a baby whose nappies had been washed into a cesspit next to the well.'),
  ('Right for the wrong reason','After the 1855 inquiry, officials still favoured miasma. London\'s great sewer system, built after the "Great Stink" of 1858, was meant to remove foul smells, but it also kept sewage out of drinking water and helped end cholera in the city.')],
 ['wiki:1854_Broad_Street_cholera_outbreak','wiki:John_Snow','wiki:Miasma_theory'],img='1854 Broad Street cholera outbreak',
 changed='1854: outbreak and pump handle removed. 1855: Snow\'s expanded study. 1858: Great Stink and sewer plans. 1884: Koch\'s work on the cholera bacterium. Since: Snow seen as a founder of epidemiology.'),
X('pasteur-germ-theory','Pasteur, Koch and germ theory: invisible life causes disease',H,'confirmed',1,1862,['experimental','scientific publication','laboratory notebooks'],
 "For centuries many people believed that life could arise spontaneously from decaying matter, and that diseases came from bad air or imbalances in the body. In the early 1860s the French chemist Louis Pasteur boiled broth in flasks with long swan-shaped necks; dust and microbes were trapped in the bend, and the broth stayed sterile, showing that microbes came from outside. In Germany Robert Koch linked specific bacteria to specific diseases, including anthrax in 1876 and tuberculosis in 1882. In 1885 Pasteur gave a rabies vaccine to Joseph Meister, a boy bitten by a rabid dog, who survived. Germ theory led to antiseptic surgery, cleaner water and many vaccines.",
 [('The private notebooks','In 1995 the historian Gerald Geison published a study of Pasteur\'s private laboratory notebooks. He argued that Pasteur sometimes presented his work more favourably in public, for example how much his rabies vaccine had been tested on animals before Meister. Others think Geison was too harsh.'),
  ('Béchamp and "terrain"','Pasteur\'s rival Antoine Béchamp argued that the state of the body, its "terrain", mattered more than outside germs. Mainstream medicine rejected his theory, but it is cited today by people who deny germ theory.'),
  ('Ideas ahead of their time','Much earlier thinkers had proposed tiny agents of disease, such as Girolamo Fracastoro in 1546. Ignaz Semmelweis\'s handwashing campaign of the 1840s was rejected in his lifetime.')],
 ['wiki:Germ_theory_of_disease','wiki:Louis_Pasteur','wiki:Spontaneous_generation'],img='Louis Pasteur',
 changed='1546: Fracastoro\'s "seeds" of contagion. 1860s: Pasteur\'s flask experiments. 1876-1882: Koch identifies anthrax and TB bacteria. 1885: rabies vaccine. 1995: Geison\'s study of the notebooks.'),
X('roentgen-xrays-1895','Röntgen\'s X-rays: seeing through flesh',H,'confirmed',2,1895,['experimental','photographic','scientific publication'],
 "On 8 November 1895 the German physicist Wilhelm Röntgen was experimenting with a cathode-ray tube in his laboratory in Würzburg when he noticed that a fluorescent screen across the room glowed, even though the tube was covered. He had found a new, invisible kind of radiation that could pass through paper, wood and flesh but was blocked by bone and metal. He called it 'X' for unknown. In December he made an X-ray image of his wife Anna Bertha's hand, showing her bones and ring. Within months doctors used X-rays to find bullets and broken bones. Röntgen received the first Nobel Prize in Physics in 1901 and chose not to patent the discovery.",
 [('Seen before but not recognised','Other researchers had probably produced X-rays without realising it. In 1890 Arthur Goodspeed in Philadelphia accidentally made an X-ray photograph and only understood it after Röntgen\'s announcement.'),
  ('Rays everywhere','The discovery set off a hunt for new rays. In 1903 French physicist René Blondlot announced "N-rays", which turned out to exist only in the eyes of the observers.'),
  ('Hidden dangers','Early users did not know X-rays were harmful. Clarence Dally, who worked on X-ray equipment for Thomas Edison, died in 1904 after severe radiation injuries. Shoe shops used X-ray machines to fit shoes until the mid-20th century.')],
 ['wiki:X-ray','wiki:Wilhelm_Röntgen','wiki:N_ray'],img='Wilhelm Röntgen',
 changed='1895: discovery. 1896: medical use spreads. 1901: first Nobel Prize in Physics. 1904: death of Clarence Dally. Since: radiation safety rules.'),
X('curie-radioactivity','Marie Curie and the glow of radium',H,'confirmed',1,1898,['experimental','scientific publication','laboratory notebooks'],
 "In 1896 Henri Becquerel found that uranium salts darkened a photographic plate even in the dark. Marie Skłodowska-Curie, a Polish-born physicist working in Paris, studied this and coined the word 'radioactivity'. With her husband Pierre she discovered two new elements in 1898, polonium (named after Poland) and radium. In 1902 Ernest Rutherford and Frederick Soddy showed that radioactive atoms change into other elements. Marie Curie shared the 1903 Nobel Prize in Physics with Pierre and Becquerel, and won the 1911 Nobel Prize in Chemistry alone, the first person to win two. She died in 1934 of aplastic anaemia, probably caused by radiation exposure. Her notebooks are still radioactive.",
 [('Nearly left out','The 1903 Nobel committee at first planned to honour only Pierre Curie and Becquerel. Pierre was told by a committee member and insisted that Marie be included.'),
  ('Modern alchemy','According to Soddy\'s later account, when he realised one element was turning into another he exclaimed that it was transmutation, and Rutherford warned him not to use the word or they would be dismissed as alchemists.'),
  ('The radium craze','In the 1910s and 1920s radium was sold as a health tonic in water, creams and toothpaste. Women who painted watch dials with radium paint, the "Radium Girls", fell ill and sued their employer, which helped bring in workplace safety laws. The US industrialist Eben Byers died in 1932 after drinking large amounts of a radium tonic.')],
 ['wiki:Marie_Curie','wiki:Radium','wiki:Radium_Girls'],img='Marie Curie',
 changed='1896: Becquerel. 1898: polonium and radium. 1903 and 1911: Nobel Prizes. 1920s: Radium Girls cases. 1934: Curie dies.'),
X('wright-flyer-1903','Kitty Hawk, 1903: the first powered flight, and its rivals',H,'confirmed',1,1903,['photographic','documentary','eyewitness'],
 "On 17 December 1903, near Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, Orville Wright flew the brothers' Wright Flyer for 12 seconds and about 37 metres. It is widely recognised as the first sustained, controlled flight of a powered, heavier-than-air aircraft with a pilot. That day the brothers made four flights; the longest, by Wilbur, lasted 59 seconds and covered about 260 metres. A famous photograph shows the first take-off. The Wrights, bicycle makers from Dayton, Ohio, had studied gliding for years, built their own wind tunnel and solved the problem of control by 'wing warping'. Within 66 years humans walked on the Moon.",
 [('Gustave Whitehead','The German-American Gustave Whitehead was reported in a 1901 newspaper article to have flown a powered aircraft in Connecticut. No photograph of the flight has been confirmed. In 2013 an editorial in Jane\'s All the World\'s Aircraft backed his claim, which most aviation historians reject.'),
  ('Santos-Dumont','In Brazil and elsewhere, Alberto Santos-Dumont is often celebrated as the true pioneer. His 14-bis flew in public in Paris in 1906 and took off on its own wheels, whereas the Wrights\' early flights had few witnesses and used a launching rail.'),
  ('A contract with the museum','When the Smithsonian Institution received the original Flyer in 1948, it signed an agreement with the Wright estate not to credit any earlier aircraft with powered, controlled flight. Supporters of rival claims point to this as a conflict of interest.')],
 ['wiki:Wright_brothers','wiki:Wright_Flyer','wiki:Gustave_Whitehead','wiki:Alberto_Santos-Dumont'],img='Wright Flyer',
 changed='1903: Kitty Hawk flights. 1906: Santos-Dumont\'s 14-bis in Paris. 1948: Flyer goes to the Smithsonian. 2013: Jane\'s editorial on Whitehead.'),
X('dna-double-helix-1953','The double helix: DNA\'s shape and Photo 51',H,'confirmed',1,1953,['experimental','X-ray crystallography','scientific publication'],
 "In April 1953 James Watson and Francis Crick, at the University of Cambridge, published in Nature a model of DNA as a double helix: two strands wound around each other, joined by paired bases. The pairing suggested how genetic information could be copied. Their model relied on X-ray diffraction work done at King's College London by Rosalind Franklin, her student Raymond Gosling and Maurice Wilkins, including a sharp image known as Photo 51, which Wilkins showed to Watson without Franklin's knowledge. Franklin died of cancer in 1958 at 37. In 1962 Watson, Crick and Wilkins received the Nobel Prize, which is not awarded after death.",
 [('Stolen or shared?','For decades Franklin was portrayed as a wronged scientist whose data were taken. In 2023 historians Matthew Cobb and Nathaniel Comfort argued in Nature, based on archival papers, that she was an equal member of a competing effort and closer to solving the structure than often assumed, rather than simply a victim.'),
  ('The race','The American chemist Linus Pauling proposed a triple-helix model early in 1953, which turned out to be wrong.'),
  ('Older roots','DNA itself was first isolated by Friedrich Miescher in 1869, and in 1944 Oswald Avery\'s team showed that it carries hereditary information.')],
 ['wiki:Nucleic_acid_double_helix','wiki:Rosalind_Franklin','wiki:Photo_51','doi:10.1038/171737a0'],img='DNA',
 changed='1869: DNA isolated. 1944: Avery shows DNA carries genes. 1952: Photo 51. 1953: double helix. 1962: Nobel Prize. 2023: new reading of Franklin\'s role.'),
X('arpanet-1969','"LO": the first message of the internet\'s ancestor',H,'confirmed',2,1969,['documentary','technological'],
 "On 29 October 1969 a student programmer, Charley Kline, at the University of California, Los Angeles, tried to log in to a computer at the Stanford Research Institute over the new ARPANET, a network funded by the US Defense Department's Advanced Research Projects Agency. He typed 'L' and 'O' before the system crashed, so the first message was 'LO'. ARPANET used packet switching, breaking data into small blocks sent independently. In 1983 it switched to the TCP/IP protocols that still underlie the internet. In 1989 Tim Berners-Lee at CERN proposed the World Wide Web, which made the internet easy for the public to use.",
 [('Built to survive a nuclear war?','A popular story says ARPANET was designed to survive a nuclear attack. Paul Baran\'s earlier work on distributed networks at the RAND Corporation was motivated by that concern, but ARPANET\'s own planners have said its main purpose was to share scarce computing resources among researchers.'),
  ('The Soviet internet that never was','In the 1960s the Soviet scientist Viktor Glushkov proposed OGAS, a nationwide computer network to manage the planned economy. It was never built, partly because of rivalries between ministries.')],
 ['wiki:ARPANET','wiki:History_of_the_Internet','wiki:OGAS'],img='ARPANET',
 changed='1969: first ARPANET link. 1983: TCP/IP. 1989: World Wide Web proposed. 1990s: public internet.'),
X('crispr-2012','CRISPR: gene editing borrowed from bacteria',H,'confirmed',1,2012,['experimental','scientific publication'],
 "In the 1990s the Spanish microbiologist Francisco Mojica, studying microbes from salt marshes near Alicante, noticed strange repeated DNA sequences, later named CRISPR. Researchers found that bacteria use them as an immune system, storing pieces of viral DNA so that an enzyme called Cas9 can recognise and cut the virus if it returns. In 2012 Jennifer Doudna and Emmanuelle Charpentier showed in Science that this system could be programmed to cut any chosen DNA sequence. Gene editing became cheaper and faster than ever before. Doudna and Charpentier received the 2020 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. In 2023 the first CRISPR-based therapy, for sickle cell disease, was approved in the United Kingdom.",
 [('The edited babies','In 2018 the Chinese scientist He Jiankui announced the birth of twin girls whose genes he had edited to try to make them resistant to HIV. The experiment was widely condemned, and he was sentenced to three years in prison in China in 2019.'),
  ('Who owns it?','The University of California and the Broad Institute, where Feng Zhang adapted CRISPR for human cells, fought a long patent battle over the technique.'),
  ('Designer humans?','Supporters see a route to curing genetic diseases. Critics worry about unintended edits, unequal access and changes that would pass to future generations.')],
 ['wiki:CRISPR','wiki:CRISPR_gene_editing','wiki:He_Jiankui_affair','doi:10.1126/science.1225829'],img='CRISPR gene editing',
 changed='1993-2005: Mojica and others study CRISPR repeats. 2012: programmable Cas9. 2018: He Jiankui affair. 2020: Nobel Prize. 2023: first approved CRISPR therapy.'),
]
# --- part 2: the nuclear and space age, near misses and pandemics
E+=[
X('enigma-bletchley','Breaking Enigma: Polish codebreakers, Bletchley Park and Alan Turing',H,'confirmed',1,1932,['documentary','declassified records','surviving machines'],
 "Germany's armed forces used Enigma cipher machines, which scrambled messages with rotating wheels and were thought to be unbreakable. In 1932 Polish mathematicians Marian Rejewski, Jerzy Różycki and Henryk Zygalski broke Enigma, and in July 1939, weeks before the German invasion of Poland, they shared their methods with British and French intelligence. At Bletchley Park in England, Alan Turing and Gordon Welchman designed electromechanical 'bombes' to find daily Enigma settings, and Tommy Flowers built Colossus, an early electronic computer, to break the German Lorenz cipher. The intelligence, codenamed Ultra, helped the Allies in the Battle of the Atlantic and elsewhere. It stayed secret until the 1970s.",
 [('How much did it shorten the war?','The official historian of British intelligence, Harry Hinsley, estimated that Ultra shortened the war in Europe by about two years. Other historians think such figures are impossible to measure.'),
  ('The Coventry story','A claim popularised in the 1970s says Churchill knew in advance that Coventry would be bombed in November 1940 and let it happen to protect the Enigma secret. Most historians reject this, saying the target was not identified in time.'),
  ('Turing\'s death','After the war Turing was prosecuted in 1952 for homosexual acts and accepted hormone treatment. He died of cyanide poisoning in 1954, and an inquest ruled suicide. Some biographers think an accident was possible. He received a posthumous royal pardon in 2013.')],
 ['wiki:Cryptanalysis_of_the_Enigma','wiki:Bletchley_Park','wiki:Alan_Turing'],img='Enigma machine',end=1945,
 changed='1932: Poles break Enigma. 1939: methods shared with Britain and France. 1974: Ultra secret made public. 2013: Turing pardoned.'),
X('hiroshima-nagasaki-1945','Hiroshima and Nagasaki: the only nuclear weapons used in war',H,'confirmed',1,1945,['documentary','photographic','survivor testimony','physical'],
 "On 6 August 1945 a US B-29 bomber dropped a uranium bomb, 'Little Boy', on the Japanese city of Hiroshima. On 9 August a plutonium bomb, 'Fat Man', was dropped on Nagasaki. Most of both cities was destroyed. Estimates of deaths by the end of 1945 range from about 90,000 to 146,000 in Hiroshima and 39,000 to 80,000 in Nagasaki, many from burns and radiation sickness. On 8 August the Soviet Union declared war on Japan, and on 15 August Emperor Hirohito announced Japan's surrender. Survivors, called hibakusha, became leading voices against nuclear weapons; their organisation Nihon Hidankyo received the Nobel Peace Prize in 2024.",
 [('Were the bombs necessary?','One view holds that the bombings ended the war quickly and avoided an invasion that would have cost more lives on both sides. Others argue that Japan was near surrender, that the Soviet declaration of war was the decisive shock, or that the bombs were partly meant to impress the Soviet Union. The debate among historians continues.'),
  ('Twice bombed','Tsutomu Yamaguchi was in Hiroshima on business on 6 August, returned home to Nagasaki and survived the second bomb too. In 2009 Japan officially recognised him as a survivor of both.')],
 ['wiki:Atomic_bombings_of_Hiroshima_and_Nagasaki','wiki:Debate_over_the_atomic_bombings_of_Hiroshima_and_Nagasaki','wiki:Tsutomu_Yamaguchi','wiki:Nihon_Hidankyo'],img='Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki',
 changed='1945: bombings and surrender. 1950s onward: hibakusha movement. 2009: Yamaguchi recognised as double survivor. 2024: Nobel Peace Prize for Nihon Hidankyo.'),
X('gagarin-1961','"Poyekhali!": Yuri Gagarin, the first human in space',H,'confirmed',1,1961,['documentary','telemetry','photographic'],
 "On 12 April 1961 the Soviet pilot Yuri Gagarin was launched from Baikonur in the spacecraft Vostok 1. As it lifted off he said 'Poyekhali!' ('Let's go!'). He made one orbit of Earth in about 108 minutes. On the way down he ejected from the capsule and landed by his own parachute in a field near Saratov, where a local woman and a young girl were the first to meet him. He became a worldwide celebrity. Gagarin died in 1968 when his MiG-15 jet crashed on a training flight; the cause has been investigated several times without agreement on every detail.",
 [('The hidden parachute landing','For years Soviet officials said Gagarin landed inside his capsule, because the rules for aviation records required a pilot to land with the craft. The truth was later admitted.'),
  ('The "Lost Cosmonauts"','Rumours, including claimed radio intercepts by two Italian amateur radio operators, the Judica-Cordiglia brothers, said the Soviet Union had secretly launched and lost cosmonauts before Gagarin. Historians who studied Soviet archives after 1991 found no evidence of such flights, though some cosmonaut trainees did die in accidents on the ground.')],
 ['wiki:Vostok_1','wiki:Yuri_Gagarin','wiki:Lost_Cosmonauts'],img='Yuri Gagarin'),
X('cuban-missile-crisis-1962','The Cuban Missile Crisis and the submarine officer who said no',H,'confirmed',1,1962,['documentary','declassified records','aerial photographs','testimony'],
 "In October 1962 American U-2 spy planes photographed Soviet nuclear missile sites being built in Cuba. President John F. Kennedy announced a naval 'quarantine' of the island, and for thirteen days the world was closer to nuclear war than ever before. On 27 October, 'Black Saturday', a U-2 was shot down over Cuba and its pilot killed. The crisis ended when Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev agreed to remove the missiles in return for a US promise not to invade Cuba and a secret agreement to withdraw American missiles from Turkey. A hotline between Washington and Moscow was set up in 1963.",
 [('Submarine B-59','On 27 October US ships dropped practice depth charges to force the Soviet submarine B-59 to surface, unaware that it carried a nuclear torpedo. According to later accounts by crew members, the captain wanted to fire it, but launching required the agreement of the senior officers aboard, and the flotilla chief of staff, Vasily Arkhipov, refused. Some historians question how close the launch really came.'),
  ('How close?','Former US defence secretary Robert McNamara later said the world avoided nuclear war partly by luck. Others argue that both leaders were more cautious than the public knew.')],
 ['wiki:Cuban_Missile_Crisis','wiki:Vasily_Arkhipov','wiki:Soviet_submarine_B-59'],img='Cuban Missile Crisis',
 changed='1962: crisis. 1963: hotline. 1990s-2000s: Soviet and US records and veterans\' accounts reveal near misses such as B-59.'),
X('apollo-13-1970','Apollo 13: the successful failure',H,'confirmed',2,1970,['documentary','telemetry','audio recordings'],
 "Apollo 13 launched on 11 April 1970 to land on the Moon. About 56 hours into the flight, on 13 April, an oxygen tank in the service module exploded, crippling the spacecraft. Astronaut Jack Swigert reported: 'Houston, we've had a problem.' The landing was abandoned. Jim Lovell, Swigert and Fred Haise used the lunar module Aquarius as a lifeboat, swung around the Moon and returned. Engineers on the ground improvised fixes, including a way to fit square carbon-dioxide filters into a round socket using tape, plastic bags and cardboard. The crew splashed down safely on 17 April. Investigators traced the explosion to damaged wiring inside the tank caused by an overlooked design change.",
 [('The number 13','Many people noted that it was Apollo 13, launched at 13:13 Houston time, and that the explosion came on 13 April. Sceptics see this as a classic example of noticing coincidences after the fact.'),
  ('"Houston, we have a problem"','The well-known form of the quote comes from the 1995 film Apollo 13; the recorded words were "we\'ve had a problem".')],
 ['wiki:Apollo_13','wiki:Apollo_13_(film)'],img='Apollo 13'),
X('petrov-1983','26 September 1983: the false alarm that did not start a nuclear war',H,'confirmed',1,1983,['documentary','testimony'],
 "Just after midnight on 26 September 1983 the Soviet early-warning system at the Serpukhov-15 command centre near Moscow reported that the United States had launched an intercontinental ballistic missile, then several more. The duty officer, Lieutenant Colonel Stanislav Petrov, judged that a real American first strike would involve hundreds of missiles and that the system was new and unreliable. He reported a false alarm rather than an attack. He was right: satellites had mistaken sunlight reflecting off high clouds for rocket launches. Tensions were very high that year: three weeks earlier the Soviet Union had shot down Korean Air Lines Flight 007. The incident was kept secret until 1998.",
 [('Did one man save the world?','Petrov was praised internationally and received several awards. Some analysts argue that Soviet procedures would not have launched a retaliation based on one officer\'s report alone. Petrov himself said he was just doing his job.'),
  ('Able Archer 83','In November 1983 a NATO exercise called Able Archer 83 alarmed some Soviet officials, who feared it could cover a real attack. Historians disagree on how close this came to war.')],
 ['wiki:1983_Soviet_nuclear_false_alarm_incident','wiki:Stanislav_Petrov','wiki:Able_Archer_83'],img='Stanislav Petrov',
 changed='1983: incident kept secret. 1998: revealed in a memoir by a former commander. 2013: Dresden Peace Prize for Petrov. 2017: Petrov dies.'),
X('challenger-1986','Challenger: seventy-three seconds and a cold O-ring',H,'confirmed',2,1986,['documentary','video','engineering analysis'],
 "On 28 January 1986 the US space shuttle Challenger broke apart 73 seconds after launch from Florida, killing all seven crew, including Christa McAuliffe, a schoolteacher chosen to be the first teacher in space. Millions watched, many children in classrooms. The Rogers Commission found that a rubber O-ring seal in the right solid rocket booster had failed in the unusually cold weather, allowing hot gas to escape. Engineers at the booster maker Morton Thiokol, including Roger Boisjoly, had warned the night before that the seals might fail in the cold, but managers approved the launch. During a public hearing the physicist Richard Feynman dipped a piece of O-ring material in iced water to show how it stiffened.",
 [('A culture problem','The sociologist Diane Vaughan described the "normalisation of deviance": small, repeated problems with the seals had come to be accepted as normal. The 2003 Columbia disaster investigation found similar organisational problems.'),
  ('Were the crew alive?','The crew cabin separated largely intact and hit the ocean minutes later. NASA could not determine whether the crew were conscious during the fall.')],
 ['wiki:Space_Shuttle_Challenger_disaster','wiki:Rogers_Commission_Report','wiki:Roger_Boisjoly'],img='Space Shuttle Challenger disaster'),
X('hiv-aids','HIV/AIDS: a new pandemic, its hidden origins and the rumours it bred',H,'confirmed',1,1981,['epidemiological','genetic','documentary'],
 "On 5 June 1981 the US Centers for Disease Control reported unusual pneumonia cases in five young gay men in Los Angeles, the first official report of what became known as AIDS. In 1983 researchers at the Institut Pasteur in Paris, including Françoise Barré-Sinoussi and Luc Montagnier, identified the virus later named HIV. Genetic studies show that HIV-1 came from a chimpanzee virus that crossed into humans in central Africa in the early 20th century, with the city of Kinshasa an early hub of spread. About 40 million people have died of AIDS-related illnesses. Since 1996 combination antiretroviral therapy has turned HIV into a manageable condition for those who can get it. Southern Africa has been among the hardest hit regions.",
 [('"Patient Zero"','A Canadian flight attendant, Gaëtan Dugas, was blamed in popular accounts as the man who brought AIDS to North America. His label in a study was "Patient O", for "outside California", misread as zero. A 2016 genetic study showed the virus was in the US years before him.'),
  ('A lab-made virus?','In the 1980s the Soviet KGB ran a disinformation campaign, known as Operation INFEKTION or Operation Denver, claiming that the US military had created AIDS. The claim spread worldwide and is still repeated. Genetic evidence points to a natural origin decades earlier.'),
  ('Denial in South Africa','Under President Thabo Mbeki, the South African government questioned whether HIV causes AIDS and delayed providing antiretroviral drugs. A 2008 Harvard study estimated that more than 330,000 lives were lost as a result. South Africa later built the world\'s largest HIV treatment programme.')],
 ['wiki:HIV/AIDS','wiki:Operation_INFEKTION','wiki:HIV/AIDS_denialism_in_South_Africa','doi:10.1126/science.1256739','doi:10.1038/nature19827'],img='HIV',
 changed='1981: first reports. 1983: virus identified. 1996: effective combination therapy. 2008: Nobel Prize for the discovery. 2014: Kinshasa origin study. 2016: Dugas cleared.'),
X('covid-19-pandemic','COVID-19: the pandemic of 2020 and the fight over its origin',H,'confirmed',1,2019,['epidemiological','genetic','documentary'],
 "In December 2019 a cluster of pneumonia cases was identified in Wuhan, China, caused by a new coronavirus, SARS-CoV-2. The World Health Organization declared a pandemic on 11 March 2020. Countries closed borders, schools and workplaces; South Africa entered one of the world's strictest lockdowns. Vaccines, including the first widely used mRNA vaccines, were approved from December 2020. In November 2021 scientists in South Africa and Botswana identified the Omicron variant. The WHO ended the global health emergency in May 2023. About 7 million deaths were officially reported, and the WHO's estimates of total excess deaths are much higher.",
 [('Market or laboratory?','Many scientists point to evidence that the virus spread to humans from animals sold at the Huanan seafood market in Wuhan, as with earlier coronaviruses. Others argue it may have escaped from research at the Wuhan Institute of Virology. US intelligence agencies have disagreed, mostly with low confidence. A WHO advisory group said in 2025 that the available evidence favoured an animal origin but that a laboratory leak could not be ruled out without more data from China.'),
  ('A wave of rumours','False claims spread widely, for example that 5G mobile networks caused the disease, which led to phone masts being set on fire in Britain. The WHO called this flood of misinformation an "infodemic".')],
 ['wiki:COVID-19_pandemic','wiki:Origin_of_SARS-CoV-2','wiki:SARS-CoV-2_Omicron_variant','wiki:COVID-19_misinformation'],img='SARS-CoV-2',end=2023,
 changed='2019: first cases. 2020: pandemic declared, lockdowns, first vaccines. 2021: Omicron identified. 2023: emergency ends. Origin still debated.'),
X('antonine-plague','The Antonine Plague: Rome\'s pandemic and the cursed casket',H,'confirmed',2,165,['textual','epigraphic','archaeological'],
 "Around 165 CE Roman troops returning from a war against the Parthian Empire in Mesopotamia brought back a deadly disease. It spread across the Roman Empire for about fifteen years, reaching Rome, Gaul and the armies on the Rhine. The physician Galen described fever, diarrhoea and a dark rash, and many historians think it was smallpox. It is named after the ruling Antonine dynasty; the co-emperor Lucius Verus died in 169, possibly of it, and Emperor Marcus Aurelius may also have died of it in 180.",
 [('The golden casket','The 4th-century historian Ammianus Marcellinus wrote that the plague began when soldiers looting a temple of Apollo at Seleucia opened a small golden casket and released a poisonous vapour. Modern historians treat this as legend that blamed the disease on sacrilege.'),
  ('An oracle against plague','The writer Lucian mocked a self-styled prophet, Alexander of Abonoteichus, whose verse "Phoebus, the unshorn, keeps off the cloud of plague" was written over doorways as protection. Lucian claimed that the houses with the verse suffered most.'),
  ('How deadly?','Estimates of deaths range widely, from a few per cent to a third of the population. Some historians see it as a turning point that weakened Rome; others think its effects were limited.')],
 ['wiki:Antonine_Plague','wiki:Galen','wiki:Alexander_of_Abonoteichus'],img='Antonine Plague',end=180),
X('polio-vaccine-1955','The polio vaccine: "Could you patent the sun?"',H,'confirmed',2,1955,['clinical trial','epidemiological','documentary'],
 "Polio epidemics paralysed or killed hundreds of thousands of children each year in the early 20th century. Jonas Salk developed an injected vaccine made from killed virus, and in 1954 it was tested on about 1.8 million American children, the 'Polio Pioneers', in one of the largest medical trials ever. On 12 April 1955 it was announced as safe and effective. Asked on television who owned the patent, Salk replied that there was none: 'Could you patent the sun?' Albert Sabin later developed an oral vaccine. A global eradication campaign began in 1988, and cases of wild polio fell by more than 99 per cent. Africa was certified free of wild polio in 2020.",
 [('The Cutter incident','In 1955 some vaccine batches made by Cutter Laboratories contained live virus. They caused about 40,000 cases of polio, left about 200 people with paralysis and killed 10. The disaster led to stricter vaccine regulation.'),
  ('Rumours and real deceptions','In 2003 some states in northern Nigeria suspended polio vaccination after claims that the vaccine caused infertility or HIV. In Pakistan, distrust grew after it was revealed in 2011 that the CIA had used a fake vaccination campaign to try to locate Osama bin Laden; polio workers have since been attacked. Wild polio remains endemic only in Pakistan and Afghanistan.')],
 ['wiki:Polio_vaccine','wiki:Jonas_Salk','wiki:Cutter_Laboratories','wiki:Polio_eradication'],img='Jonas Salk',
 changed='1954: field trial. 1955: vaccine announced; Cutter incident. 1960s: oral vaccine. 1988: global eradication campaign. 2020: Africa free of wild polio.'),
]
# --- part 3: Greek myths and their afterlives, Eden, and Venda's sacred lake
E+=[
X('orpheus-eurydice','Orpheus and Eurydice: the singer who must not look back',M,'myth-legend',2,-530,['textual','archaeological','iconographic'],
 "Orpheus was the greatest musician of Greek myth, whose songs could charm animals, trees and stones. When his wife Eurydice died of a snakebite, he went down to the underworld and his music moved Hades and Persephone to let her return, on one condition: he must not look back at her until they reached the world above. Just before the exit he turned, and she vanished for ever. Orpheus is mentioned as famous by the 6th century BCE; the fullest surviving versions of the descent are by the Roman poets Virgil and Ovid. A religious movement, Orphism, claimed his teachings on the soul and life after death.",
 [('Instructions for the dead','Thin gold tablets found in graves in southern Italy, Greece and Crete, from about the 4th century BCE onward, give the dead directions for the underworld, such as which spring to drink from. Many scholars link them to Orphic or similar mystery beliefs. The Derveni papyrus, a partly burned Orphic commentary from about 340 BCE, is often called Europe\'s oldest surviving manuscript.'),
  ('Don\'t look back','Stories of a forbidden backward glance appear elsewhere: Izanagi in Japan, and Lot\'s wife in Genesis. Researchers have also collected many Native North American tales of a man who travels to the land of the dead to bring back his wife. Some see a shared ancient story, others a common human idea.'),
  ('Happy ending?','Some scholars argue that earlier Greek versions may have let Orpheus succeed in rescuing Eurydice, and that the tragic ending was added later. The evidence is thin.')],
 ['wiki:Orpheus','wiki:Orphism_(religion)','wiki:Derveni_papyrus'],img='Orpheus',dateNote='Orpheus named by the 6th century BCE; descent story told in full by Virgil (29 BCE) and Ovid (8 CE)'),
X('persephone-demeter','Persephone and Demeter: the kidnapped daughter and the hungry earth',M,'myth-legend',2,-650,['textual','archaeological'],
 "In the Homeric Hymn to Demeter, probably composed in the 7th or 6th century BCE, Hades, god of the underworld, seizes Persephone, the daughter of the grain goddess Demeter. Grieving, Demeter searches the earth and stops crops from growing, until Zeus orders Persephone's return. But Persephone has eaten pomegranate seeds in the underworld, so she must spend part of every year there. The hymn links the story to the founding of the Eleusinian Mysteries, secret rites at Eleusis near Athens that promised initiates a better fate after death.",
 [('Winter or summer?','The story is often read as an explanation of winter. Some scholars argue that in the Greek climate her absence may match the hot, dry summer, when grain was stored underground in jars before autumn sowing.'),
  ('Descents elsewhere','The Sumerian goddess Inanna also goes down to the underworld and must leave a substitute there for part of the year. Whether these myths are related or simply share a common theme of seasons and death is debated.')],
 ['wiki:Persephone','wiki:Homeric_Hymn_to_Demeter','wiki:Demeter'],img='Persephone'),
X('pandoras-jar','Pandora: the first woman and the jar of evils',M,'myth-legend',2,-700,['textual','iconographic'],
 "In the poems of Hesiod, around 700 BCE, Zeus punishes humans after Prometheus steals fire for them. He has the smith god Hephaestus make the first woman, Pandora ('all-gifted'), and the gods give her beauty and cunning. She opens a large jar and releases sickness, toil and other evils into the world; only Hope stays inside. In Hesiod the container is a pithos, a storage jar. It became a 'box' after the 16th-century scholar Erasmus rendered it with a word meaning a small box.",
 [('A blame myth?','Many modern readers see the story as blaming women for human suffering, much like later readings of Eve in Genesis. Others note that the two stories differ in many details.'),
  ('An older goddess?','The scholar Jane Ellen Harrison argued in the early 20th century that Pandora, whose name can also mean "all-giving", was originally an earth goddess who gave gifts, and that Hesiod turned her into a source of evil. This remains a theory.'),
  ('What is Hope doing in the jar?','Scholars disagree whether keeping Hope in the jar means it was kept for humans as a comfort, or kept from them.')],
 ['wiki:Pandora','wiki:Pandora\'s_box'],img='Pandora'),
X('labours-of-heracles','The twelve labours of Heracles and the Pillars at the edge of the world',M,'myth-legend',2,-700,['textual','iconographic','archaeological'],
 "Heracles (Hercules to the Romans), son of Zeus and a mortal woman, was driven mad by the goddess Hera and killed his own children. To atone, he served King Eurystheus and performed a series of near-impossible tasks, usually counted as twelve: among them, killing the Nemean lion and the many-headed Hydra, cleaning the stables of King Augeas in one day by diverting rivers, fetching golden apples from the garden of the Hesperides and bringing the three-headed dog Cerberus up from the underworld. After death he became a god. The rocks on either side of the Strait of Gibraltar were called the Pillars of Heracles, marking the edge of the known world; Plato placed Atlantis beyond them.",
 [('Heracles and Melqart','Greek writers identified Heracles with Melqart, the god of the Phoenician city of Tyre. Herodotus visited his temple at Tyre, and a famous temple of Melqart stood at Gades (Cádiz) near the Strait of Gibraltar. Some historians think the Pillars legend reflects Phoenician sailors\' sanctuaries.'),
  ('A journey through the zodiac?','From the late 18th century some writers, such as Charles-François Dupuis, argued that the twelve labours stood for the Sun passing through the twelve signs of the zodiac. Most scholars reject this, noting that ancient lists of the labours varied.')],
 ['wiki:Labours_of_Hercules','wiki:Heracles','wiki:Pillars_of_Hercules','wiki:Melqart'],img='Labours of Hercules'),
X('odyssey-homer','The Odyssey: ten years to get home, and the hunt for the real Ithaca',M,'myth-legend',1,-725,['textual','archaeological','astronomical'],
 "Homer's Odyssey, composed probably in the late 8th or 7th century BCE, follows the Greek hero Odysseus on his ten-year journey home from the Trojan War. He outwits the one-eyed Cyclops Polyphemus, resists the Sirens' song by having himself tied to the mast, escapes the witch Circe, steers between the monster Scylla and the whirlpool Charybdis, and visits the land of the dead. He finally returns in disguise to Ithaca, where his wife Penelope has held off a crowd of suitors. The poem has shaped Western storytelling ever since.",
 [('Where was Ithaca?','Homer\'s description of Ithaca does not fit the modern island well. In 2005 Robert Bittlestone and colleagues argued that Homer\'s Ithaca was Paliki, a peninsula of neighbouring Kefalonia once separated by a channel. Many archaeologists remain unconvinced.'),
  ('An eclipse in the poem?','In 2008 two researchers argued in PNAS that astronomical hints in the Odyssey match the solar eclipse of 16 April 1178 BCE, around the time Odysseus would have returned. Critics say the hints are too vague to date the story.'),
  ('Who was Homer?','Scholars debate whether "Homer" was one poet, several or a long oral tradition. In 1897 the novelist Samuel Butler even argued the Odyssey was written by a young woman in Sicily, an idea few accept.')],
 ['wiki:Odyssey','wiki:Homeric_Question','doi:10.1073/pnas.0803317105','doi:10.1353/apa.2012.0006'],img='Odyssey',dateNote='poem composed c. late 8th–7th century BCE; set after the Trojan War'),
X('daedalus-icarus','Daedalus and Icarus: wings of wax and the first inventor',M,'myth-legend',2,-700,['textual','iconographic','experimental'],
 "In Greek myth Daedalus was a brilliant Athenian craftsman who built the Labyrinth for King Minos of Crete to hold the Minotaur. When Minos kept him prisoner, Daedalus made wings of feathers and wax for himself and his son Icarus. He warned Icarus not to fly too low, where the sea would soak the feathers, nor too high, where the sun would melt the wax. Icarus flew too high, the wax melted and he fell into the sea, which was named the Icarian Sea after him. Daedalus is mentioned in Homer's Iliad; the best-known version of the flight is in Ovid's Metamorphoses.",
 [('Living statues','Ancient writers credited Daedalus with statues so lifelike that they seemed to move; Plato joked that they had to be tied down to stop them running away. Some modern writers cite this as an early idea of robots.'),
  ('Flying from Crete','In 1988 the human-powered aircraft Daedalus 88, built by a team from MIT and pedalled by the Greek cyclist Kanellos Kanellopoulos, flew about 115 km from Crete to Santorini, a record for human-powered flight.'),
  ('Too close to the sun?','Readers have noted that air gets colder, not hotter, at greater heights. The myth is usually read as a lesson about ambition and the limits of technology rather than physics.')],
 ['wiki:Daedalus','wiki:Icarus','wiki:MIT_Daedalus'],img='Icarus',dateNote='Daedalus named in Homer\'s Iliad; flight story best known from Ovid (8 CE)'),
X('cassandra','Cassandra: the prophet no one believed',M,'myth-legend',2,-700,['textual','iconographic'],
 "Cassandra was a princess of Troy. In one version, the god Apollo gave her the gift of prophecy in hope of winning her love; when she refused him, he cursed her so that no one would ever believe her predictions. She warned the Trojans that the wooden horse left by the Greeks was a trap, but they brought it inside the walls. After the fall of Troy she was taken as a captive by King Agamemnon and murdered with him by his wife Clytemnestra, as told in Aeschylus' play Agamemnon (458 BCE).",
 [('The Cassandra syndrome','Today "a Cassandra" means someone whose accurate warnings are ignored. The label has been applied to many real cases, such as engineers who warned before disasters, and scientists warning about pandemics or climate change. Critics note that people who warned wrongly are rarely remembered, so the pattern can be exaggerated after the fact.')],
 ['wiki:Cassandra','wiki:Agamemnon_(play)'],img='Cassandra'),
X('oedipus','Oedipus: the prophecy that made itself come true',M,'myth-legend',2,-700,['textual'],
 "An oracle told King Laius of Thebes that his son would kill him and marry his wife. Laius had the baby abandoned on a mountainside, but the child, Oedipus, was rescued and raised in Corinth. As a young man he heard the same prophecy and fled Corinth to avoid it. On the road he killed a stranger, who was Laius. He freed Thebes from the Sphinx by answering its riddle and married the widowed queen, Jocasta, his own mother. When the truth emerged, Jocasta killed herself and Oedipus blinded himself. Sophocles' play Oedipus the King, of about 429 BCE, is the most famous version.",
 [('The Oedipus effect','Every attempt to escape the prophecy helped fulfil it. The philosopher Karl Popper used the term "Oedipus effect" for predictions that influence the events they predict, and the sociologist Robert Merton described the "self-fulfilling prophecy".'),
  ('The Oedipus complex','Sigmund Freud took the name for his theory that young children have hidden desires toward one parent and rivalry with the other. The theory is widely disputed by modern psychologists.')],
 ['wiki:Oedipus','wiki:Oedipus_Rex','wiki:Self-fulfilling_prophecy','wiki:Oedipus_complex'],img='Oedipus',dateNote='myth older than Homer; Sophocles\' play c. 429 BCE'),
X('sisyphus','Sisyphus: the king who cheated death',M,'myth-legend',3,-700,['textual','iconographic'],
 "Sisyphus, the cunning king of Corinth, tricked the gods more than once. In one story he chained up Thanatos, Death himself, so that no one on earth could die until the war god Ares freed him. In another, he told his wife not to bury him properly, then persuaded Persephone to let him return to the living to complain, and refused to go back. As punishment he was condemned in the underworld to roll a huge boulder up a hill forever; each time it neared the top it rolled down again. Homer's Odyssey describes him at his endless task.",
 [('Happy Sisyphus','In 1942 the French writer Albert Camus used the myth in his essay The Myth of Sisyphus as a picture of human life without ultimate meaning, ending: "One must imagine Sisyphus happy."'),
  ('Allegory','The Roman poet Lucretius read Sisyphus as a symbol of the politician who keeps seeking power and never finds satisfaction.')],
 ['wiki:Sisyphus','wiki:The_Myth_of_Sisyphus'],img='Sisyphus'),
X('garden-of-eden','The Garden of Eden: a paradise with an address?',M,'myth-legend',1,-600,['textual','geographical'],
 "In the Book of Genesis, God plants a garden in Eden 'in the east' and places the first humans, Adam and Eve, there. A river flows out of Eden and divides into four: the Pishon, the Gihon, the Tigris and the Euphrates. A serpent persuades Eve to eat fruit from the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, which God had forbidden; she and Adam are expelled, and death and hard labour enter human life. Scholars generally date the written text to the first millennium BCE, drawing on older Near Eastern traditions. The story is central to Judaism, Christianity and Islam.",
 [('Under the Persian Gulf?','The archaeologist Juris Zarins argued that Eden could be a memory of a fertile area at the head of the Persian Gulf, where the Tigris, Euphrates and other rivers met before rising sea levels flooded it after the last Ice Age. Most scholars treat Eden as theology rather than geography.'),
  ('Many Edens','People have placed Eden in many locations. In 1881 the British general Charles Gordon suggested the Vallée de Mai on Praslin in the Seychelles. Early Latter-day Saint teaching places it in Jackson County, Missouri.'),
  ('Shared themes','Scholars compare Eden with the Sumerian paradise of Dilmun, with Gilgamesh losing the plant of immortality to a snake, and with Pandora in Greek myth.')],
 ['wiki:Garden_of_Eden','wiki:Adam_and_Eve','wiki:Dilmun'],img='Garden of Eden',dateNote='written text usually dated to the 1st millennium BCE'),
X('lake-fundudzi','Lake Fundudzi: the Venda sacred lake guarded by a python',M,'myth-legend',2,1900,['oral tradition','geological','ethnographic'],
 "Lake Fundudzi lies in the Soutpansberg mountains of Limpopo, South Africa. Geologists explain it as a lake formed when an ancient landslide blocked the Mutale River; it is often described as one of the very few natural freshwater lakes in southern Africa. The lake, its crocodiles and the nearby Thathe Vondo forest are sacred to the Vhatavhatsindi, the 'People of the Pool', a group within the Venda people. In Venda tradition the lake is protected by a python god, and the colour and fullness of the water are said to show the mood of the ancestors and the chance of rain. The python god is honoured each year in a puberty dance in which adolescent girls move in a long, swaying line.",
 [('The drowned village','A Venda legend says the lake formed when a village refused food and shelter to a passing traveller, a man with leprosy, who cursed it; the village sank beneath the new lake, and in the early morning people say the sound of drums, voices and cattle can be heard from the water. Stories of a place destroyed for refusing hospitality to a stranger occur elsewhere too, such as the Greek tale of Baucis and Philemon.'),
  ('Science and the sacred','The landslide origin is the geological explanation. For the Vhatavhatsindi the lake remains a place of the ancestors, and the two accounts answer different questions.')],
 ['wiki:Lake_Fundudzi','wiki:Venda_people'],img='Lake Fundudzi',dateNote='oral tradition, recorded in the 20th century; lake formed in prehistory'),
]
